WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.743
(bouncy music)

00:00:08.902 --> 00:00:09.735
(swooshing)

00:00:09.735 --> 00:00:10.568
(hinge creaking)

00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:13.151
(bouncy music)

00:00:13.842 --> 00:00:15.430
^(bell rings)

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:18.550
^- Artie from Glee is played by an actor

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.980
who's this incredible dancer,

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.543
which feels very ironic to me.

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.720
I get it when you're casting a big name so that the show

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.110
or the movie is getting more reviews

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.063
or you have a name behind it, but this kid,

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.593
I think was an unknown.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.890
- I appreciate that the show is trying to promote diversity

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.603
by having a disabled character like Artie,

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.400
but I think he could have possibly been handled

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.770
in a little more of a sensitive way.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:50.040
- So he's having some sort of, a dream of sorts,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.543
where he's dancing in the mall.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.780
I mean, you can dance in a wheelchair.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.220
- I think they were trying to show how much he wants

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.140
to be like how he was before he became disabled.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:01.460
And that's real.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.460
But I don't think portraying that through a dance sequence

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:11.020
where he's suddenly so sad to be back in his chair again,

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.040
and he's back to being disabled again,

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.003
I don't think that is the right way to show that.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.750
- I'm picturing every kid at home who has a disability

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.923
who watched that and was like, feeling like,

00:01:22.057 --> 00:01:23.850
"Should I be dreaming about not having this?"

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.390
Or, "Should I be wishing that I don't have this?"

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.840
Instead of, "How do I love what makes me me?"

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:30.830
- And I think it would have been a lot more powerful

00:01:30.830 --> 00:01:32.610
to maybe show him going through

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:36.353
an arc where he reaches self-acceptance and self-love,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.833
rather than trying to change his circumstances.

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:40.740
- You know, he plays guy in wheelchair.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.620
And guy in wheelchair wants to walk.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:45.900
Ooo, what a shocking story arc.

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:47.370
It doesn't offend me anymore, I just feel

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.520
like it's lazy writing.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:52.570
- If they had a writer or maybe somebody there

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.170
with the experience that Artie has,

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.520
^that scene would have been treated differently.

00:01:58.557 --> 00:01:59.557
^- Hi, Becky.

00:02:01.018 --> 00:02:01.889
^About Friday night.

00:02:01.889 --> 00:02:03.389
^- Are you excited?

00:02:03.561 --> 00:02:05.130
- Well, I kind of thought Becky was the Band-Aid.

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.837
Becky was them being like, "We messed up.

00:02:06.837 --> 00:02:08.887
"We messed up the first time around."

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.510
- I think any casting of any disabled actor,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.143
I am all for it.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:16.250
- I loved Becky from Glee.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.083
I love her.

00:02:17.083 --> 00:02:19.280
She is a character with Down Syndrome

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.100
who also has Down Syndrome in real life.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:24.263
And her character was dynamic and funny,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.383
and sometimes a bitch, and I loved that.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:29.770
Sometimes people with disabilities are bitches.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:31.113
Sometimes I'm a bitch.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:33.843
It just felt authentic and real.

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:35.540
- I don't think we should date.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.564
- Is it because I'm too intimidating?

00:02:39.564 --> 00:02:40.397
(laughs)

00:02:40.397 --> 00:02:41.230
- I love her.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:42.133
- Yeah.

00:02:42.133 --> 00:02:45.170
- Okay, so this plays into a whole 'nother thing.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:46.003
Whole 'nother?

00:02:46.003 --> 00:02:46.836
Whole other?

00:02:46.836 --> 00:02:47.669
Doesn't matter.

00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:50.160
People with disabilities date, but we don't always have

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.003
^to date people with disabilities.

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:55.010
^- Auggie, I'm sorry, but you're not the only one

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.623
^who has bad days.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:02:58.393
^- Bad days?

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:00.657
^Do people avoid touching you?

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.020
^When a person accidentally touches you,

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.370
^do they call it the plague?

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:07.750
- So, I just watched "Wonder," and I wanted to love it,

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.873
and I really struggled with the movie.

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:13.270
- I'm glad that they validate the struggle

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.180
of going through an experience like disability

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.983
that is so isolating.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:23.033
It's difficult feeling like an outsider.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:25.427
- I remember being like, "Please give another kid the award,

00:03:25.427 --> 00:03:27.120
"please give another kid the award."

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.720
- We shouldn't just be getting awards

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.403
for basically living our life.

00:03:31.541 --> 00:03:33.352
- I won a character award when I was in high school.

00:03:33.352 --> 00:03:34.185
I was like, "What?"

00:03:34.185 --> 00:03:35.018
(snorts)

00:03:35.018 --> 00:03:35.906
(laughs)

00:03:35.906 --> 00:03:37.323
Just sort of the same thing.

00:03:37.323 --> 00:03:39.156
Yay, you're different!

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.790
- I don't know, I think it's a little bit tricky

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:46.100
to have a disabled character that gets an award

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:50.210
because they're disabled, but I love that it's more because

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:53.190
of the courage and the heart that they have.

00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:55.913
- Inspiration porn, this is inspiration porn.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.500
Everyone's brave, we're all doing things that scare us,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.023
disability or otherwise.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.003
^- I am not to be pitied, I'm here, and I'm hard to kill.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.373
^- [Woman] How's it coming in there?

00:04:07.114 --> 00:04:07.947
^- Fine.

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.883
- RJ Mitte, oh good, yay.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.770
- For me, Walter Jr. is a perfect example

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:15.810
of a character that represents the community.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.340
- He's an incredible actor.

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.283
He's incredible.

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.870
And I don't say an incredible actor with a disability,

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:22.813
he's just an incredible actor.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:23.960
- I was just really excited.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.580
I remember watching the show for the first time,

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.310
and hearing about his being cast

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.330
and that he has cerebral palsy, and I have cerebral palsy.

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:35.410
I've never seen, this was the first actor that I have seen

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:39.113
of my age that I've gotten to watch.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:40.663
- Oh, this is from the pilot.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.187
Oh yeah, I remember this as a kid.

00:04:42.187 --> 00:04:44.270
(laughs)

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:46.133
- I feel that.

00:04:46.133 --> 00:04:46.966
- [Woman] Do you want me, or your dad?

00:04:46.966 --> 00:04:49.283
- Getting stuck in a dressing room, ugh!

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:50.413
It's the worst.

00:04:50.448 --> 00:04:51.281
- Dad.

00:04:51.281 --> 00:04:53.553
- And I remember that too, when I first became an amputee.

00:04:53.553 --> 00:04:58.553
It wasn't like it was easily readily available

00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:02.570
for me to get dressed or how to figure it out,

00:05:02.570 --> 00:05:03.470
how to get dressed.

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:06.460
- This is actually something similar that happened with me

00:05:06.460 --> 00:05:08.250
in terms of finding coats and stuff.

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:10.720
- I don't know, in a super teeny-tiny way,

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.460
it's very very silly, but I love

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:17.120
that we have similar hands somehow, or we move similarly,

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.320
I don't know, I just think that's very cool.

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:20.349
You don't see that.

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:22.550
Or I don't see that very much.

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.100
I don't know, I would just watch him,

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.557
and be like, "We move similarly.

00:05:25.557 --> 00:05:28.570
"We kind of, we get through similar situations."

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:29.500
It's validating.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:30.760
- It's three-dimensional.

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.050
We at least see this kid as part of a family,

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:35.460
as part of his world, and his storyline again,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.950
^isn't just disability.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:39.913
^- You're hiding, huh?

00:05:41.365 --> 00:05:42.940
^(laughs)

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.100
^I know that.

00:05:44.314 --> 00:05:45.940
(laughs)

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.333
- This is gonna be fun.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.940
- Arnie from What's Eating Gilbert Grape, Leonardo DiCaprio.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:54.820
I loved this movie as a kid, but I re-watched it as an adult

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:58.960
and I struggled with how they justified

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.883
having Leonardo DiCaprio play a kid with a disability.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:05.300
- Well, it's like watching old World War II films

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:07.127
that are completely racist, and you're just like,

00:06:07.127 --> 00:06:10.693
"Wow, the way they represent this group of people, and that,

00:06:10.747 --> 00:06:12.087
"it's insulting."

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.350
- I do agree, I think it's definitely a product of it's time

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.550
and I think we're still kind of working on trying

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:21.890
to figure out what's acceptable in terms

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:24.453
of who should we cast to play certain roles.

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:25.610
- [Narrator] Doctor said we'd be lucky

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:26.883
if Arnie lived to be 10.

00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:30.803
- Doctor said we'd be lucky if Arnie lived to be 10.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.013
It's clear that he's not gonna die by 10.

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:35.000
So they start the movie out

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:36.780
with some pretty big misconceptions.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.240
People assume disability means shorter lifespan,

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.903
and I think things like this don't help that narrative.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:46.120
- This character is sort of served up on a platter

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:47.967
for able-bodied viewers of being like,

00:06:47.967 --> 00:06:52.240
"Feel bad for this family for having this kind of child."

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.423
^- I'm guilty of being a dwarf.

00:06:56.470 --> 00:06:58.653
^- You're not on trial for being a dwarf.

00:06:58.930 --> 00:07:01.840
^- Oh, yes I am.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.323
^I've been on trial for that my entire life.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.800
- Yeah, there it is, Tyrion Lannister, that's the shit.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:12.170
- I have such a crush on this man, such a crush on this man.

00:07:12.170 --> 00:07:14.670
- There's a lot of things with Tyrion that I relate to

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:15.963
almost immediately.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:20.510
- So so often we see little people being cast in the role

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.713
of comedic, the comedy role.

00:07:22.763 --> 00:07:25.343
Enough with the lollipop guild.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.730
I love that this is a drama, that there is more

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:29.943
to his storyline.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:33.440
- I love how intelligent he is and how conniving he is,

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:34.833
and he's so funny.

00:07:35.050 --> 00:07:37.250
- That is exactly the representation

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:38.700
that you're looking for.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:43.130
That kind of validity, that kind of understanding

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:44.770
of what a community's going through,

00:07:44.770 --> 00:07:46.390
that's the thing that we're talking about.

00:07:46.390 --> 00:07:49.690
- So often the storyline around someone with a disability

00:07:49.690 --> 00:07:52.903
is disability, like, that's the storyline.

00:07:53.042 --> 00:07:54.740
And I think when they cast someone

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:57.140
who actually has a disability, almost always,

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.683
we get a more dynamic story.

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:02.340
- When I first became an amputee, I'd watch YouTube videos

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.193
on above and below knee amputees running or swimming,

00:08:07.334 --> 00:08:11.053
and it made me feel like I could have a life.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:15.983
If I could see someone who looks like me and is an amputee,

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:17.440
that would mean the world to me,

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:18.940
like that would be so amazing.

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:22.033
- Growing up, I never really had a movie, like,

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:24.620
I don't even remember if there were movies made

00:08:24.620 --> 00:08:26.530
about disabled people when I was growing up.

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:29.544
Forrest Gump, and then Hunchback of Notre Dame.

00:08:29.544 --> 00:08:31.733
Those were the only two that I remember.

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.343
- We want to be able to watch our stories,

00:08:34.430 --> 00:08:37.777
and relate to them, and not sort of cringe, and go,

00:08:37.777 --> 00:08:39.627
"Okay, well, this wasn't exactly written for us.

00:08:39.627 --> 00:08:42.330
"It was written, inspire other people."

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:43.740
We want to be inspired.

00:08:43.740 --> 00:08:46.120
We want to be inspired by our own gifts,

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.760
and our own talents, and our own stories and struggles,

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.500
and everything else, like, everybody else gets the privilege

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.273
to go to a movie and see themselves.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:58.170
So I think we're making great steps as of right now,

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.290
but I think we can go a little bit further

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:04.023
in including more people with disabilities in those stories.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:06.263
(swooshing)

00:09:06.263 --> 00:09:07.865
(hinge creaking)

